## What to check

**Does this belong in the ref tier at all.** The escape hatch stays
narrow by review discipline as much as by grammar (v5 §4.5). If the
draft's `<body>` is really a technical decision, it belongs in
comparch or subcomparch; if it's really a standing rule, it belongs in
policy; if it's really naming and defining a term, it belongs in
vocab. A ref that is a dedicated tier's content wearing a `kind`
attribute is a blocking finding — name which tier it actually belongs
to.

**Is `kind` the closest honest fit.** Check `runbook`, `style_guide`,
`impl_guide`, `app_prompt`, and `mocks` before accepting `other` — a
draft that reaches for `other` when a specific kind plainly applies
has left the next reader to open the body just to find out what it
is.

**Has the body stayed general.** The grammar allows only `<body>` —
no sub-tags, no per-kind structure. Flag any invented internal
structure (a private heading scheme, a format that only makes sense
for one kind) as the accretion v5 §4.5 warns against.

**If `kind="mocks"`, check the extraction discipline.** Mock content
is evidence, not something to transcribe. A body that reads like a
verbatim copy of mock code or markup rather than a description or
reference is a blocking finding — the fresh-scaffold rule is being
violated at the source. Separately, check whether the body only
describes the happy path the mock shows: a `mocks` ref that doesn't
propose the missing empty, error, loading, and denied states has left
the negative-space work undone, even if what it does describe is
accurate.

Return a `<review verdict="approve|revise">` with a `<summary>` and
one `<finding severity="blocking|advisory">` per issue — each
`<location>` naming the attribute or the body region at fault, and
each `<comment>` saying what's wrong and what a passing version would
contain.
